  {
   "key": "what_exists",
   "title": "What exists already",
   "text": "A paper reports that Krabbe UK is a charity that supports families affected by Krabbe disease [Ed349bff]. A paper reports that KrabbeConnect, based in the United States, works to connect Krabbe disease science with families [E7aa1953]. According to NIH RePORTER, the National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke funds a grant working on replacing certain brain cells for Krabbe and related diseases [E4d8b50c, E5332e1a]. According to NIH RePORTER, this same institute also funds a grant studying how loss of the GALC gene leads to Krabbe disease [Ecfe2466, Eaa7364b]. According to ClinicalTrials.gov, there is a trial recruiting in the United States that is testing umbilical cord blood derived cells given into the spinal fluid for Krabbe and related metabolic diseases [E478a4f9, E66c8c49]. According to ClinicalTrials.gov, another trial testing a treatment called PBKR03 for early infantile Krabbe disease had sites in several countries but is currently paused [E84bed90, E8e004b3]. According to ClinicalTrials.gov, a newer trial plans to test a treatment called PLX-200 in children with Krabbe disease, though it has not started recruiting yet [E2aaf128, Ec260f91]. A paper reports that there is also a Krabbe Disease Global Patient Registry that collects information to help improve the accuracy of diagnosis [Ecbb1cae, E92ff8aa].",
